<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">
Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Training
<br class="ltx_break">for VLA Flow Models
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hongyin Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shiyuan Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Junxi Jin
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qixin Zeng
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yifan Qiao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hongchao Lu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Donglin Wang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author. wangdonglin@westlake.edu.cn</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models based on flow matching have shown excellent performance in general-purpose robotic manipulation tasks.
However, the action accuracy of these models on complex downstream tasks is unsatisfactory.
One important reason is that these models rely solely on the post-training paradigm of imitation learning, which makes it difficult to have a deeper understanding of the distribution properties of data quality, which is exactly what Reinforcement Learning (RL) excels at.
In this paper, we theoretically propose an offline RL post-training objective for VLA flow models and induce an efficient and feasible offline RL fine-tuning algorithm <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="--" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">--</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptive <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>einforced <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>low <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>atching (<span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ARFM</span>).
By introducing an adaptively adjusted scaling factor in the VLA flow model loss, we construct a principled bias-variance trade-off objective function to optimally control the impact of RL signal on flow loss.
ARFM adaptively balances RL advantage preservation and flow loss gradient variance control, resulting in a more stable and efficient fine-tuning process.
Extensive simulation and real-world experimental results show that ARFM exhibits excellent generalization, robustness, few-shot learning, and continuous learning performance.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>
Westlake University, Hangzhou, China</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>
University of California, Los Angeles, USA</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>
Xi’an Jiaotong University, Xi’an, China</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, the rapid development of vision-language-action (VLA) models has enabled robots to perform a variety of manipulation tasks based on multi-modal perception.
Large-scale pre-training systems have demonstrated the feasibility of learning general policies from internet-level multi-modal data and have performed well in real-world robotic manipulation tasks, such as RT-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brohan et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, RT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brohan et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, PaLM-E <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Driess et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, Octo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
In particular, <math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a VLA flow policy model based on trajectory vector fields, which utilizes trajectory-level denoising modeling mechanism to achieve parallel and efficient policy learning and achieves state-of-the-art performance in realistic manipulation tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although pre-trained VLA models show good generalizability, fine-tuning on downstream tasks based on the imitation learning paradigm still faces great challenges in terms of action accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brohan et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
In this scenario, VLA models that rely solely on behavior cloning or flow matching may not be able to effectively exploit the quality distribution structure in the training data.
Some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mark et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> resort to offline Reinforcement Learning (RL) in the hope of mining deeper and fine-grained data quality features to achieve more efficient VLA model fine-tuning.
Recently, an algorithm called ReinboT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> was proposed, which attempts to introduce RL return-to-go as a fine-grained goal to guide the fine-tuning of the VLA model.
However, we observed that this approach has limited performance in VLA flow models (such as <math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), see the experimental section for details.
We infer that this is because VLA flow models the entire action trajectory distribution through a vector field.
During the inference phase of the VLA flow model, the maximized return-to-go can only control the generation of the trajectory vector field, thereby indirectly and inefficiently guiding the final action prediction.
Therefore, how to effectively perform offline RL fine-tuning of VLA flow models remains underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose <span id="Sx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptive <span id="Sx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>einforced <span id="Sx1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>low <span id="Sx1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>atching (<span id="Sx1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ARFM</span>), a novel adaptive offline RL fine-tuning method for VLA flow models.
We control the strength of the RL signal introduced into the sample data by introducing an adaptively adjusted scaling factor in the VLA flow loss function.
Specifically, we theoretically construct a variance-bias trade-off optimization objective with respect to the scaling factor.
We aim to dynamically select the scaling factor to preserve the RL signal, i.e., samples with higher RL advantage are still amplified in the data distribution during flow model fine-tuning.
Meanwhile, we hope to control the flow matching loss gradient variance, i.e., avoid some weights from exponentially exploding, resulting in excessive gradient variance and training crash.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we focus on adaptively balancing “retaining enough RL advantage signal” and “controlling loss gradient variance” in the scaling factor as the distribution of the current batch data changes during the post-training process of the VLA flow model.
We theoretically construct an optimization objective for the scaling factor and construct an approximately analyzable objective function through some reasonable assumptions.
A bisection iterative algorithm for real-time updating of the scaling factor is further induced, so that the loss of the VLA flow model is adaptively adjusted and corrected according to the data distribution.
Our principled contributions include three aspects:</p>
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose ARFM, a novel offline RL post-training method for VLA flow models that can adaptively adjust the data quality distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We theoretically establish the optimization objective of adaptively adjusting the scaling factor and induce a bisection iterative algorithm to update the factor in real time, thereby achieving efficient VLA flow model fine-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments in simulation and real-world robot manipulation tasks show that our proposed ARFM exhibits state-of-the-art generalization ability, robustness to dynamic perturbations, and excellent performance in few-shot and continuous learning scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flow matching models in RL.</span> Flow matching (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al. 2023</a></cite>) is a more general counterpart to the diffusion model (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho, Jain, and Abbeel 2020</a></cite>).
Both diffusion and flow matching tend to have good results in offline RL.
Some previous work explored modeling behavior policies based on diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janner et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang, Hunt, and Zhou 2022</a>)</cite>.
Following these studies, some researchers modeled offline RL objectives as energy-guided diffusion processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, while others adopted the same policy optimization approach utilizing diffusion and flow matching models without classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ajay et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. 2023</a>)</cite>.
Recently, an energy-weighted flow matching method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang, Zhang, and Gu 2025</a>)</cite> for learning energy-guided distributions was proposed, which can directly utilize RL rewards or other metrics as energy functions without learning intermediate energy models.
Compared with previous studies, our work further extends the energy-weighted flow matching method to the VLA model post-training setting and proposes a new adaptive energy-weighted algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RL for VLA models.</span> RL has recently emerged as a pivotal technique for post-training VLA models, overcoming the limitations of vanilla imitation learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mark et al. 2024</a>)</cite> on infrastructure such as RT-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brohan et al. 2022</a>)</cite>.
These efforts are mainly divided into online and offline RL fine-tuning.
Online methods exploit direct environment interaction, adopt algorithms such as PPO (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al. 2017</a></cite>) or develop more data-efficient interaction frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.
On the other hand, offline methods learn from static datasets by introducing various signals, such as human preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and value guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakamoto et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
Recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> has effectively implemented the core idea of RL in predicting the future to maximize return when examining offline RL post-training of VLA models.
The idea of utilizing RL returns as fine-grained goals to guide the policy comes from the previous new paradigms of sequence modeling, Decision Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2021</a>)</cite> and Reinformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
Different from these studies, our work considers the implementation of offline RL post-training in the energy-weighted VLA flow model to achieve efficient and stable policy optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first list the energy-weighted flow matching and give two equivalent loss functions to train a neural network policy to approximate the energy-guided flow.
We then list the RL advantage evaluation method for post-training data and the expressive VLA flow model.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Energy-weighted flow matching</h3>

<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both flow matching (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al. 2023</a></cite>) and Energy-Weighted Flow Matching (EWFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang, Zhang, and Gu 2025</a>)</cite> learn the <span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vector field</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2019</a>)</cite>. Consider a probability density path: <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\mapsto p_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\mapsto p_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the <span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vector field</span> <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}:[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which <span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generates</span> the probability density path <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}p_{t}(\mathbf{x})+\mbox{div}(\mathbf{v}_{t}(\mathbf{x})p_{t}(\mathbf{x}))=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}p_{t}(\mathbf{x})+\mbox{div}(\mathbf{v}_{t}(\mathbf{x})p_{t}(\mathbf{x}))=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the conditional distribution <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional vector field is defined in the same way.
Consider a probability distribution <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and an energy function <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the energy-guided distribution satisfies:</p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\propto p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\propto p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then consider adding Gaussian noises as conditional distributions to the two distributions: <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})=q_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0}):=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})=q_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0}):=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}):=\int_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}):=\int_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\mathbf{x}):=\int_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}q_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\mathbf{x}):=\int_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}q_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as the marginal distributions at time <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, then energy-weighted flow matching aims to efficiently learn the vector field <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> which generates <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span> <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Energy-weighted flow matching ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> calculates the vector field <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> which generates <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span> <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ Energy-weighted flow matching ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> finds the equivalent loss between learning the conditional vector field and the marginal vector field.
The proof process is in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in Eq. (<a href="#Sx3.E2" title="In Energy-weighted flow matching ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and the conditional distributions <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0}),q_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0}),q_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, marginal distributions <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}),q_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}),q_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> are also defined above. Consider the conditional vector field <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> which generates <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the vector field <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> which generates <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have:
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})=\int_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}p_{0t}(\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})\frac{\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))}{\exp(-\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x}))}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})=\int_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}p_{0t}(\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})\frac{\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))}{\exp(-\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x}))}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is an intermediate energy function which is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="Sx3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x})=-\log\mathbb{E}_{p_{0t}(\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x})}[\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x})=-\log\mathbb{E}_{p_{0t}(\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x})}[\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span> <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Energy-weighted flow matching ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> finds out a closed form of the vector field <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, but it still remains challenging to learn <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, since the intermediate energy function is unknown during the process, therefore <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span> <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ Energy-weighted flow matching ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> gives the method to simplify the training process.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),p_{t}(\mathbf{x}),q_{t}(\mathbf{x}),\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}),\\
\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),q_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),p_{t}(\mathbf{x}),q_{t}(\mathbf{x}),\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}),\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}),\\
\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are all defined above.
Consider learning a model <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> with the learnable parameter <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, then we define the Energy-weighted Flow Matching (EFM) loss <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{EFM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{EFM}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="Sx3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{EFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x},t}[\frac{\exp(-\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x}))}{\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim p_{t}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}})}[\exp(-\mathcal{E}_{t}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}))]}||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})||^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{EFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x},t}[\frac{\exp(-\mathcal{E}_{t}(\mathbf{x}))}{\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim p_{t}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}})}[\exp(-\mathcal{E}_{t}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}))]}||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{t}(\mathbf{x})||^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the Conditional Energy-weighted Flow Matching (CEFM) loss <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CEFM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CEFM}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="Sx3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{CEFM}(\theta)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{CEFM}(\theta)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="Sx3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x},t}[\frac{\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))}{\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim p_{0}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{0})}[\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{0}))]}||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})||^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x},t}[\frac{\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_{0}))}{\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim p_{0}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{0})}[\exp(-\beta\mathcal{E}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{0}))]}||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})||^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the expectation on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is taken over some predefined distribution <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the data distribution <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled by <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> with conditional distribution <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the flow <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t0}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the two losses are equal up to a constant factor to <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,</span></p>
<table id="Sx3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{EFM}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{CEFM}(\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{EFM}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{CEFM}(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span> <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ Energy-weighted flow matching ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> proves that the training process of the vector field of the marginal energy-guided distribution could be simplified to learning the conditional vector field, without the calculation of the intermediate energy function.
In the VLA offline RL post-training process, given the observation and action actor <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and compared with the vanilla imitation learning action distribution <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider designing a new energy function <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and further extend the EWFM method to fine-tune the energy-guided VLA model distribution <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\propto p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\exp(\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\propto p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\exp(\beta\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RL advantage signal estimation</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Leave-one-out <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kool, van Hoof, and Welling 2019</a>)</cite> estimates the RL advantage using sampling.
The REINFORCE Leave-One-Out generates <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> independent samples <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{K}\sim p_{\theta}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{K}\sim p_{\theta}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> and utilizes all other samples to compute a baseline for the current return:
<math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(c,x_{k})=R(c,x_{k})-\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{i=1,i\neq k}^{K}R(c,x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(c,x_{k})=R(c,x_{k})-\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{i=1,i\neq k}^{K}R(c,x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The equivalent form of this objective is:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(c,x_{k})=\frac{K}{K-1}\left(R(c,x_{k})-\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}R(c,x_{i})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(c,x_{k})=\frac{K}{K-1}\left(R(c,x_{k})-\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}R(c,x_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is a simple, unbiased, and low-variance advantage estimate, so we are inspired by it to design a critic-free offline RL advantage signal for the VLA multi-task setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">VLA flow model</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. 2024</a>)</cite> is a recently proposed VLA flow model with excellent performance, which mainly consists of a language model transformer backbone.
Following the standard late fusion VLM recipe, image encoders embed the robot’s image observations into the same embedding space as language tokens.
The observation <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of multiple RGB images, a language command, and the robot’s proprioceptive state. Formally, <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{t}=[I_{1}^{t},\dots,I_{n}^{t},\ell^{t},q^{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{t}=[I_{1}^{t},\dots,I_{n}^{t},\ell^{t},q^{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th image, <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of language tokens, and <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector of joint angles.
The action chunk <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=[\mathbf{a}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t+1},\dots,\mathbf{a}_{t+H-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=[\mathbf{a}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t+1},\dots,\mathbf{a}_{t+H-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a sequence of future actions, where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the action horizon.
The Flow Matching (FM) loss is given by:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t}),q(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})}\left[\left\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})\right\|^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t}),q(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})}\left[\left\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})\right\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s output, <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the denoising vector field, and <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\tau\mathbf{A}_{t},(1-\tau)I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\tau\mathbf{A}_{t},(1-\tau)I)</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The noisy actions <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed as <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}=\tau\mathbf{A}_{t}+(1-\tau)\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}=\tau\mathbf{A}_{t}+(1-\tau)\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
During inference, actions are generated by integrating the learned vector field from <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>, starting with random noise <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{0}^{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{0}^{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The integration rule is given by:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\tau+\delta}^{t}=\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}+\delta\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\tau+\delta}^{t}=\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t}+\delta\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{\tau}^{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the integration step size.
It is worth noting that our adaptive offline RL post-training theoretical analysis and the induced fine-tuning algorithm can be applied but not limited to the <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04063v1/figures/pipeline.png" id="Sx3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/420;" width="698" height="420" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The proposed ARFM method.
We study the offline RL post-training process of the VLA flow model, where the model input includes language instructions, external image perception and proprioception, and action noise that needs to be restored. We theoretically construct an energy-weighted flow matching loss with adaptive scaling factor, aiming to balance the RL signal and gradient variance on the data samples. Finally, we establish a solvable optimization objective and obtain a nonlinear equation about the scaling factor, which is finally solved by bisection iteration.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Methodology</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work aims to construct a novel efficient post-training method for the VLA Flow model and induce a practical offline RL fine-tuning algorithm, as shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ VLA flow model ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
First, we construct an energy-weighted VLA model and the corresponding energy-weighted flow matching loss.
Then, we explain in detail how to construct the optimization objective of the adaptive scaling factor in the loss function, which aims to weigh the RL signal and gradient variance of the data samples.
Finally, we give the solution equations of the scaling factor under some reasonable assumptions, as well as the corresponding bisection iteration and the fine-tuning algorithm of the VLA flow model.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Energy-weighted VLA flow model</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a data distribution <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=[\mathbf{a}_{t},\dots\mathbf{a}_{t+H}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=[\mathbf{a}_{t},\dots\mathbf{a}_{t+H}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the action chunk, and <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the observation containing images in different views, language tokens, and a vector of joint angles. The aim is to sample from the energy-guided distribution, which is:</p>
<table id="Sx4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\propto p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\propto p(\mathbf{A}_{t}|\mathbf{o}_{t})\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is scaling factor, and <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the return-to-go advantage. We could learn the vector field of the policy distribution <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing CEFM loss:</p>
<table id="Sx4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{E}^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}^{\tau}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}^{\tau}_{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})||^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{E}^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}^{\tau}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}^{\tau}_{t}|\mathbf{A}_{t})||^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="Sx4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{where }\mathcal{E}^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})=\frac{\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}{\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{*}\sim p(\cdot|\mathbf{o}_{t})}\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{*},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{where }\mathcal{E}^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})=\frac{\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}{\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{*}\sim p(\cdot|\mathbf{o}_{t})}\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{*},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here in the expectation in <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, the action <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the distribution <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\cdot|\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\cdot|\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is to add a simple linear Gaussian noise (Optimal Transport) on <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}=\tau\mathbf{A}_{t}+(1-\tau)\mathbf{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}=\tau\mathbf{A}_{t}+(1-\tau)\mathbf{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard Gaussian variable. <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional vector field, which could be formulated by: <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t})=\epsilon-\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t})=\epsilon-\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practical post-training, we utilize the sampling assumption instead of directly calculating the expectation.
In each step, we sample a batch of <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> data pairs of <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{o}_{t},\mathbf{A}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and calculate the softmax of the energy function as the energy weight <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then calculate the weighted loss <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> within this batch, so the practical loss function should be:</p>
<table id="Sx4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}_{1}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{B}w_{i}(\alpha)||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}_{1}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{B}w_{i}(\alpha)||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="Sx4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{where }w_{i}(\alpha)=\frac{\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}{\sum_{j=1}^{B}\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{j},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{where }w_{i}(\alpha)=\frac{\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}{\sum_{j=1}^{B}\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{j},\mathbf{o}_{t}))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are a batch sampled from <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\cdot|\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\cdot|\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noisy action from <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We utilize the standardized return <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the energy weight in the loss function <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}_{1}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}_{1}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The standardization here is performed according to the same task type in the VLA multi-task setting to facilitate the comparison of RL advantages of different states (by utilizing Eq. <a href="#Sx3.E4" title="In RL advantage signal estimation ‣ Preliminaries ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Moreover, <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in the loss function <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}_{1}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}_{1}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is an important adaptive parameter, and we adaptively adjust its value by constructing an optimization objective that trades off between RL signal and gradient variance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor <math id="Sx4.SSx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The scaling factor <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> plays an important role in post-training of the VLA flow model.
Intuitively, if <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is too small, the advantage of the data sample may not be fully reflected, so the improvement on the original flow matching is not significant.
When <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, energy-weighted fine-tuning degenerates into vanilla flow matching.
When <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is large, fine-tuning tends to focus only on those data samples with higher energy.
This will cause the sample data variance to be too large, resulting in gradient explosion and thus destroying the stability of fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the scaling factor <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is adaptively adjusted in each fine-tuning step, and its adjustment direction is to minimize <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Sx4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\alpha)=\mbox{Var}(\hat{g}(\alpha))-\lambda S(\alpha)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\alpha)=\mbox{Var}(\hat{g}(\alpha))-\lambda S(\alpha),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}(\alpha)=\nabla_{\theta}L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{B}\hat{w}_{i}(\alpha)\nabla_{\theta}||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}(\alpha)=\nabla_{\theta}L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{B}\hat{w}_{i}(\alpha)\nabla_{\theta}||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau},\mathbf{o}_{t})-\mathbf{u}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gradient of the loss <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> tends to minimize the variation of the gradient. <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\alpha)=\sum_{i=1}^{B}\hat{w}_{i}(\alpha)R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t})/\sum_{i=1}^{B}\hat{w}_{i}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\alpha)=\sum_{i=1}^{B}\hat{w}_{i}(\alpha)R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t})/\sum_{i=1}^{B}\hat{w}_{i}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is a score function which shows the effect of the RL advantage as the energy weight, and <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> tends to maximize the score. <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{w}_{i}=\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{w}_{i}=\exp(\alpha R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math> is the energy weight. <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter, to adjust the ratio between the RL signal and the gradient variance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Bisection Iteration of Scaling Factor <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="alg1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom"><span id="alg1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> RL advantages <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, FM losses <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{FM}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{FM}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, hyperparameter <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, value range of <math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha_{min},\alpha_{max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{min},\alpha_{max}]</annotation></semantics></math>, number of iterations <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and tolerance <math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_figure_panel ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
             Calculate <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{R}^{2}=\sum_{i}R_{i}^{2}/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{R}^{2}=\sum_{i}R_{i}^{2}/B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> =<math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}L^{i}_{FM}/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}L^{i}_{FM}/B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{L}^{2}=\sum_{i}(L_{FM}^{i}-\mu_{L})^{2}/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{L}^{2}=\sum_{i}(L_{FM}^{i}-\mu_{L})^{2}/B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{low}=\sigma_{A}^{2}\alpha_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{low}=\sigma_{A}^{2}\alpha_{min}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{high}=\sigma_{A}^{2}\alpha_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{high}=\sigma_{A}^{2}\alpha_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
             Define <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)=4\sqrt{x}e^{2x}-2\sqrt{x}e^{x}-\frac{\lambda\sigma_{R}}{\sigma_{L}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)=4\sqrt{x}e^{2x}-2\sqrt{x}e^{x}-\frac{\lambda\sigma_{R}}{\sigma_{L}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{mid}=0.5(x_{low}+x_{high})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{mid}=0.5(x_{low}+x_{high})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F(x_{mid})|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F(x_{mid})|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>: <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Break</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x_{mid})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x_{mid})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{high}=x_{mid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{high}=x_{mid}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>: <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{low}=x_{mid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{low}=x_{mid}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
             <span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}=\sqrt{0.5(x_{low}+x_{high})}/\sigma_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}=\sqrt{0.5(x_{low}+x_{high})}/\sigma_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
             <span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return</span> clip (<math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*},\alpha_{max},\alpha_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*},\alpha_{max},\alpha_{min}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
</div></div></div>
</figure>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> ARFM: Post-Training of VLA Flow Model</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="alg2.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom"><span id="alg2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span>: Post-traing data <math id="alg2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, VLA flow model <math id="alg2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_figure_panel ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
             <span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> a batch of data <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              <span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[B]</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
               Sample <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\sim\mbox{Uniform}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Uniform</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\sim\mbox{Uniform}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
               <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}=\tau(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})+(1-\tau)\epsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}=\tau(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})+(1-\tau)\epsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
               <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}=R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}=R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}=\exp(R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}=\exp(R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
               <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{FM}^{i}=||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau},\mathbf{o}_{t})-(\epsilon-\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{FM}^{i}=||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau},\mathbf{o}_{t})-(\epsilon-\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              <span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              Calculate the optimal <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor 𝛼 ‣ Methodology ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}(\alpha^{*})=\exp(\alpha^{*}g_{i})/\sum_{j}\exp(\alpha^{*}g_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}(\alpha^{*})=\exp(\alpha^{*}g_{i})/\sum_{j}\exp(\alpha^{*}g_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)=\sum_{i}w_{i}(\alpha^{*})L_{FM}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)=\sum_{i}w_{i}(\alpha^{*})L_{FM}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              Take a gradient step of <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}^{\tau}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
              
             <span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, minimizing <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is to balance the trade-off to emphasize the energy weight and prevent the post-training from gradient explosion.
In order to make this optimization objective solvable, three assumptions need to be considered:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The standardized RL advantage signal <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{o}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be Gaussian variable <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) loss <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{CFM}^{i}=||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i})-\mathbf{u}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{CFM}^{i}=||\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i},\mathbf{o}_{t}^{i})-\mathbf{u}(\{\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i}\}^{\tau}|\mathbf{A}_{t}^{i})||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is also assumed to be Gaussian variable <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu_{L},\sigma_{L}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu_{L},\sigma_{L}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When the batch size <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough, the sample-based expectation and variance could be utilized to approximate <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{L},\sigma_{A},\sigma_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{L},\sigma_{A},\sigma_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the post-training stage of the VLA flow model, the RL advantage is standardized, and the CFM loss value will quickly tend to have a lower variance, so the Gaussian distribution assumption here is mild and reasonable.
Based on these assumptions, we can obtain two important corollaries on the solution of the scaling factor <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
The derivation details are in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With the assumptions and functions defined above, the objective <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> could be represented by:</span></p>
<table id="Sx4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(\alpha)=\sigma_{L}^{2}[e^{2\alpha^{2}\sigma_{R}^{2}}-e^{\alpha^{2}\sigma_{R}^{2}}]-\lambda\alpha\sigma_{R}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(\alpha)=\sigma_{L}^{2}[e^{2\alpha^{2}\sigma_{R}^{2}}-e^{\alpha^{2}\sigma_{R}^{2}}]-\lambda\alpha\sigma_{R}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> which minimizes <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is solved by:
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="Sx4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 4\sqrt{x^{*}}e^{2x^{*}}-2\sqrt{x^{*}}e^{x^{*}}-\frac{\lambda\sigma_{R}}{\sigma_{L}^{2}}=0,\alpha^{*}=\frac{\sqrt{x^{*}}}{\sigma_{R}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msqrt><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 4\sqrt{x^{*}}e^{2x^{*}}-2\sqrt{x^{*}}e^{x^{*}}-\frac{\lambda\sigma_{R}}{\sigma_{L}^{2}}=0,\alpha^{*}=\frac{\sqrt{x^{*}}}{\sigma_{R}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary</span> <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor 𝛼 ‣ Methodology ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives a specific solvable optimization objective for <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary</span> <a href="#Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 2. ‣ Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor 𝛼 ‣ Methodology ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> gives the corresponding nonlinear equation for <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be quickly solved utilizing bisection iteration (Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor 𝛼 ‣ Methodology ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Therefore, we can utilize Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor 𝛼 ‣ Methodology ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to obtain the optimal <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby obtaining the loss based on energy-weighted flow matching.
This adaptive loss function is finally utilized in the post-training process of the VLA flow model (Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Adaptive adjustment of the scaling factor 𝛼 ‣ Methodology ‣ Balancing Signal and Variance: Adaptive Offline RL Post-Trainingfor VLA Flow Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section,
we conduct extensive experiments in various scenarios to evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed ARFM method.
Specifically, we aim to examine the following five questions:
<span id="Sx5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1)</span> Does ARFM show superior generalization ability compared to previous SOTA baseline methods?
<span id="Sx5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2)</span> How resistant is ARFM to action noise in robot manipulation tasks with action noise?
<span id="Sx5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3)</span> How does ARFM perform in data-scarce scenarios and lifelong learning, especially in terms of few-shot learning and continuous learning capabilities?
<span id="Sx5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4)</span> To what extent do the key hyper-parameters in the ARFM method affect the performance of the VLA flow model?
<span id="Sx5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5)</span> How does ARFM perform in real-world robot manipulation tasks, especially manipulating disturbed objects?</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04063v1/figures/exp_setting_1.png" id="Sx5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/361;" width="698" height="361" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>We evaluate performance on four categories of the LIBERO benchmark suite (Object, Long, Spatial and Goal) and three categories of real-world UR5 grasping and placing tasks.
In realistic experiments, we introduce multiple external perturbations to the objects to be manipulated.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx5.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx5.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 15.0pt;">  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></td>
<td id="Sx5.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 15.0pt;"></td>
<td id="Sx5.T1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 15.0pt;" colspan="5">  <span id="Sx5.T1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LIBERO Multi-task Learning</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx5.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
